+   1. lowered to L - L/16 if above it (the margin);
+   2. if step 1 lowered it, raised to L/64 above what the run left if below
+      that, so a heap whose live part is inside the margin is walked again
+      only after that much growth;
+   3. lowered to L - L/256 if above it, so the last stretch before the
+      limit still has a run. This can leave it below the heap, when the
+      run left more than that.
+   A run the limit scheduled (steps 1 or 3 lowered the threshold) also
+   waits until the heap has grown by L/256 from its least since the last
+   run (js_trigger_gc): one that found the heap all live is not repeated at
+   every object made, and a heap that shrank and filled again with garbage
+   is collected however full the last run left it. So a heap that is all
+   live reaches the limit in at most 8 runs once it is inside the margin:
+   the runs come at least min(L/64, the distance to L - L/256) of growth
+   apart, which L/16 / (L/64) + (L/64) / (L/256) bounds. */
+static void gc_schedule(JSRuntime *rt)
+{
